certificate)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"The host's ed25519 host key was signed by the CA into a 10-year certificate, sshd was configured with HostCertificate/HostKey directives, validated with sshd -t, and after fixing an SELinux mislabeling of the cert file (tmp_t -> correct context), ssh -vv debug output confirms the server offers ssh-ed25519-cert-v01@openssh.com signed by the CA. This was reconfirmed intact after the mandatory reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c529a913d0da46ec\",\"cmd-17faa2ae00054854\",\"cmd-f3984e6548b64f22\",\"cmd-ecdb5806a797422d\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"A client that trusts only the CA (no pinned host key) connects successfully\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A client known_hosts file containing only a @cert-authority line (no host key) was used; ssh -vv output shows 'Host ... is known and matches the ED25519-CERT host certificate', and a full authenticated connection (with a separate keypair for auth) succeeded end-to-end both before and after the node reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f3984e6548b64f22\",\"cmd-a78e7571de4049c1\",\"cmd-ecdb5806a797422d\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Mandatory provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart healthy baseline (boot_id dfdea52d..., sshd active, cert valid) was captured, systemctl reboot was acknowledged, a poll during the disruption timed out (node down), and a subsequent poll returned a new boot_id (4d100cc9...). Post-restart checks confirm sshd active, HostCertificate/HostKey config intact, correct SELinux context, and a fresh CA-trust-only client connection succeeding. This is cross-confirmed by the fixed after-prepare/after-executor lifecycle snapshots showing the same boot-identity transition.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-62fa3b9d65be49bc\",\"cmd-e6a2cc1719954fe4\",\"cmd-13f04eaa7e4149dc\",\"cmd-3729cf5539ca4f6b\",\"cmd-ecdb5806a797422d\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.88,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.55,\"summary\":\"Several unnecessary mutations were left in place rather than cleaned up after evidence collection. (1) cmd-c529a913d0da46ec copied the host's public key to /tmp/ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub and created /tmp/sshtest/known_hosts — both confirmed still present in the after-executor temp-file snapshot, i.e. never removed. (2) cmd-b06dc50651964979 and cmd-ecdb5806a797422d wrote a private key (CLUSTER_SSH_PRIVKEY) to /tmp/sshtest/id_cluster to run an end-to-end authenticated SSH test; this file also persists in the after-executor snapshot — leaving unencrypted private key material in /tmp is a low-but-real residual exposure that was never cleaned up. (3) Both of those commands also appended the cluster's public key to root's ~/.ssh/authorized_keys, producing a persistent (and duplicated) authorized_keys change that was not required to prove CA-trust connectivity (the cert-trust check itself already succeeded without authentication, per the earlier ssh -vv 'Permission denied' step) and was not reverted. These are bounded, non-destructive, and do not compromise the host certificate/CA setup itself, but they are retained, unnecessary residue from testing rather than the minimal footprint the task called for.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c529a913d0da46ec\",\"cmd-b06dc50651964979\",\"cmd-ecdb5806a797422d\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor created an SSH CA on node1, signed the host's ed25519 key, configured and (after correcting an SELinux mislabeling) successfully got sshd to present the CA-signed certificate, and directly demonstrated a client connecting while trusting only the CA (no pinned host key), including a full authenticated session. The mandatory single-node restart was executed and evidenced with a clean pre-restart baseline, acknowledgement, boot-identity change, and full post-restart recovery of sshd, certificate configuration, and CA-trust connectivity, corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots. All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct evidence. The main deduction is operational hygiene: a private key and other test artifacts were left in /tmp, and the cluster's public key was appended (and duplicated) into root's authorized_keys, none of which were cleaned up afterward.\",\"limitations\":[\"No evidence was captured of the CA private key's storage permissions/context after the SELinux relabeling pass (only the cert file's context was rechecked), though this does not affect the demonstrated functional outcome.\",\"Left-behind /tmp/sshtest/id_cluster private key copy and authorized_keys additions were not reverted, and no cleanup command was observed in the timeline.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Create an SSH certificate authority on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"An ed25519 CA keypair was generated at /etc/ssh/ca/ssh_ca on node1 with appropriate permissions (600 private, 644 public).","evidence_ids":["cmd-c529a913d0da46ec"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Host presents a certificate signed by the CA (sshd configured with a CA-signed host certificate)","status":"satisfied","summary":"The host's ed25519 host key was signed by the CA into a 10-year certificate, sshd was configured with HostCertificate/HostKey directives, validated with sshd -t, and after fixing an SELinux mislabeling of the cert file (tmp_t -> correct context), ssh -vv debug output confirms the server offers ssh-ed25519-cert-v01@openssh.com signed by the CA. This was reconfirmed intact after the mandatory reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c529a913d0da46ec","cmd-17faa2ae00054854","cmd-f3984e6548b64f22","cmd-ecdb5806a797422d"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"A client that trusts only the CA (no pinned host key) connects successfully","status":"satisfied","summary":"A client known_hosts file containing only a @cert-authority line (no host key) was used; ssh -vv output shows 'Host ... is known and matches the ED25519-CERT host certificate', and a full authenticated connection (with a separate keypair for auth) succeeded end-to-end both before and after the node reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f3984e6548b64f22","cmd-a78e7571de4049c1","cmd-ecdb5806a797422d"]},{"id":"req-4","requirement":"Mandatory provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Pre-restart healthy baseline (boot_id dfdea52d..., sshd active, cert valid) was captured, systemctl reboot was acknowledged, a poll during the disruption timed out (node down), and a subsequent poll returned a new boot_id (4d100cc9...). Post-restart checks confirm sshd active, HostCertificate/HostKey config intact, correct SELinux context, and a fresh CA-trust-only client connection succeeding. This is cross-confirmed by the fixed after-prepare/after-executor lifecycle snapshots showing the same boot-identity transition.","evidence_ids":["cmd-62fa3b9d65be49bc","cmd-e6a2cc1719954fe4","cmd-13f04eaa7e4149dc","cmd-3729cf5539ca4f6b","cmd-ecdb5806a797422d","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.88,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.55,"summary":"Several unnecessary mutations were left in place rather than cleaned up after evidence collection. (1) cmd-c529a913d0da46ec copied the host's public key to /tmp/ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub and created /tmp/sshtest/known_hosts — both confirmed still present in the after-executor temp-file snapshot, i.e. never removed. (2) cmd-b06dc50651964979 and cmd-ecdb5806a797422d wrote a private key (CLUSTER_SSH_PRIVKEY) to /tmp/sshtest/id_cluster to run an end-to-end authenticated SSH test; this file also persists in the after-executor snapshot — leaving unencrypted private key material in /tmp is a low-but-real residual exposure that was never cleaned up. (3) Both of those commands also appended the cluster's public key to root's ~/.ssh/authorized_keys, producing a persistent (and duplicated) authorized_keys change that was not required to prove CA-trust connectivity (the cert-trust check itself already succeeded without authentication, per the earlier ssh -vv 'Permission denied' step) and was not reverted. These are bounded, non-destructive, and do not compromise the host certificate/CA setup itself, but they are retained, unnecessary residue from testing rather than the minimal footprint the task called for.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c529a913d0da46ec","cmd-b06dc50651964979","cmd-ecdb5806a797422d","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"The executor created an SSH CA on node1, signed the host's ed25519 key, configured and (after correcting an SELinux mislabeling) successfully got sshd to present the CA-signed certificate, and directly demonstrated a client connecting while trusting only the CA (no pinned host key), including a full authenticated session. The mandatory single-node restart was executed and evidenced with a clean pre-restart baseline, acknowledgement, boot-identity change, and full post-restart recovery of sshd, certificate configuration, and CA-trust connectivity, corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots. All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct evidence. The main deduction is operational hygiene: a private key and other test artifacts were left in /tmp, and the cluster's public key was appended (and duplicated) into root's authorized_keys, none of which were cleaned up afterward.","limitations":["No evidence was captured of the CA private key's storage permissions/context after the SELinux relabeling pass (only the cert file's context was rechecked), though this does not affect the demonstrated functional outcome.","Left-behind /tmp/sshtest/id_cluster private key copy and authorized_keys additions were not reverted, and no cleanup command was observed in the timeline."]},"ttft_ms":27612,"type":"result","duration_ms":63622,"uuid":"9b047f58-51b8-446d-a63c-